1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,990
Right now, you're spinning at hundreds of

2
00:00:01,990 --> 00:00:03,119
miles an hour.

3
00:00:03,119 --> 00:00:04,941
Now imagine it all stops.

4
00:00:04,941 --> 00:00:06,294
In one second.

5
00:00:06,294 --> 00:00:09,137
Winds four times stronger than any hurricane.

6
00:00:09,137 --> 00:00:11,305
Waves taller than skyscrapers.

7
00:00:11,305 --> 00:00:12,785
And a day that lasts a whole

8
00:00:12,785 --> 00:00:14,837
year. So what would really happen?

9
00:00:18,667 --> 00:00:19,227
Right now,

10
00:00:19,227 --> 00:00:20,227
as you watch this,

11
00:00:20,227 --> 00:00:22,864
you're spinning at hundreds of miles an hour

12
00:00:22,864 --> 00:00:24,531
And you can't feel a thing

13
00:00:24,531 --> 00:00:24,531
The Earth turns once a day,

14
00:00:26,151 --> 00:00:27,391
and everything on it,

15
00:00:27,391 --> 00:00:27,751
you,

16
00:00:27,751 --> 00:00:28,271
the air,

17
00:00:28,271 --> 00:00:29,031
the oceans,

18
00:00:29,031 --> 00:00:30,148
turns with it

19
00:00:30,148 --> 00:00:30,148
Every sunrise and every sunset,

20
00:00:32,328 --> 00:00:33,928
every day and every night,

21
00:00:33,928 --> 00:00:35,776
comes from this one spin

22
00:00:35,726 --> 00:00:35,776
So what would happen if,

23
00:00:37,076 --> 00:00:38,935
one second from now...

24
00:00:38,935 --> 00:00:40,523
the Earth just stopped?

25
00:00:40,523 --> 00:00:40,523
The answer is terrifying

26
00:00:42,431 --> 00:00:42,911
And very,

27
00:00:42,911 --> 00:00:44,187
very strange

28
00:00:44,187 --> 00:00:44,187
So let's find out

29
00:00:45,850 --> 00:00:46,100
We're going to stop the planet,

30
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:48,840
and watch what happens,

31
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:50,600
second by second

32
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:54,680
First,

33
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:56,846
how fast are we really spinning?

34
00:00:56,846 --> 00:00:59,366
The Earth makes one full turn every twenty-four

35
00:00:59,366 --> 00:01:00,435
hours

36
00:01:00,165 --> 00:01:00,435
Well,

37
00:01:00,975 --> 00:01:01,886
almost

38
00:01:01,886 --> 00:01:02,846
Strictly speaking,

39
00:01:02,846 --> 00:01:05,246
one full turn takes about twenty-three hours

40
00:01:05,246 --> 00:01:07,006
and fifty-six minutes

41
00:01:06,886 --> 00:01:07,156
At the equator,

42
00:01:08,016 --> 00:01:10,296
that means the ground under your feet is moving

43
00:01:10,296 --> 00:01:12,896
at about one thousand miles per hour

44
00:01:12,752 --> 00:01:13,022
That's faster than the speed of sound

45
00:01:15,217 --> 00:01:17,617
Faster than most passenger jets

46
00:01:17,425 --> 00:01:17,695
Even in New York or Paris,

47
00:01:19,235 --> 00:01:21,515
you're moving at more than six hundred miles

48
00:01:21,515 --> 00:01:22,683
per hour

49
00:01:22,413 --> 00:01:22,683
Only at the North and South Poles are you

50
00:01:24,823 --> 00:01:26,853
simply turning on the spot

51
00:01:26,583 --> 00:01:26,853
And on top of that,

52
00:01:27,993 --> 00:01:30,273
the Earth races around the Sun at about

53
00:01:30,273 --> 00:01:32,673
sixty-seven thousand miles per hour

54
00:01:32,785 --> 00:01:33,055
So why don't we feel any of it?

55
00:01:35,158 --> 00:01:35,998
Because the ground,

56
00:01:35,998 --> 00:01:38,598
the air and the oceans all move together

57
00:01:38,576 --> 00:01:38,996
It's like sitting in a smooth airplane

58
00:01:41,296 --> 00:01:43,216
You only feel the speed when it suddenly

59
00:01:43,216 --> 00:01:44,336
changes

60
00:01:44,116 --> 00:01:44,386
And that's exactly the problem...

61
00:01:46,745 --> 00:01:49,145
if the Earth ever stopped

62
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:54,120
So let's do it

63
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:55,965
Imagine the planet stops

64
00:01:55,965 --> 00:01:57,085
Instantly

65
00:01:57,183 --> 00:01:57,403
Not over days

66
00:01:58,927 --> 00:02:00,430
Not over hours

67
00:02:00,430 --> 00:02:02,304
In a single second

68
00:02:02,034 --> 00:02:02,304
The ground stops

69
00:02:03,808 --> 00:02:04,128
But you,

70
00:02:04,128 --> 00:02:04,568
the air,

71
00:02:04,568 --> 00:02:05,896
and the oceans?

72
00:02:05,896 --> 00:02:07,470
You keep going

73
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:07,470
This is called inertia

74
00:02:09,206 --> 00:02:10,806
Things that are moving keep moving,

75
00:02:10,806 --> 00:02:12,886
unless something stops them

76
00:02:12,653 --> 00:02:12,923
Near the equator,

77
00:02:14,063 --> 00:02:16,343
anything not bolted to solid rock would be

78
00:02:16,343 --> 00:02:18,943
thrown east at a thousand miles per hour

79
00:02:19,014 --> 00:02:19,234
That's about four times faster than the fastest

80
00:02:21,814 --> 00:02:23,077
race car

81
00:02:22,807 --> 00:02:23,077
It would be like a car crash

82
00:02:24,921 --> 00:02:26,975
For the entire planet

83
00:02:26,795 --> 00:02:26,975
People,

84
00:02:27,515 --> 00:02:30,115
cars and animals would be flung sideways

85
00:02:30,246 --> 00:02:30,516
Buildings,

86
00:02:31,136 --> 00:02:31,576
trees,

87
00:02:31,576 --> 00:02:33,176
even the top layer of soil,

88
00:02:33,176 --> 00:02:35,019
would be ripped away

89
00:02:34,599 --> 00:02:35,019
And the closer you were to the equator,

90
00:02:36,999 --> 00:02:38,485
the worse it would be

91
00:02:38,485 --> 00:02:40,605
But the real destruction is only getting

92
00:02:40,605 --> 00:02:41,725
started

93
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:47,560
Because the air above you would keep rushing

94
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:48,786
east too

95
00:02:48,516 --> 00:02:48,786
Air isn't glued to the ground

96
00:02:50,673 --> 00:02:52,664
So it would keep its speed

97
00:02:52,394 --> 00:02:52,664
That means winds far stronger than any

98
00:02:54,924 --> 00:02:56,684
hurricane in history

99
00:02:56,471 --> 00:02:56,741
The fastest wind gust ever measured was about

100
00:02:59,401 --> 00:03:01,682
two hundred and fifty miles per hour

101
00:03:01,682 --> 00:03:03,042
These winds?

102
00:03:03,042 --> 00:03:05,122
About four times faster

103
00:03:04,877 --> 00:03:05,147
Winds like that would shred almost anything

104
00:03:07,487 --> 00:03:09,452
humans have ever built

105
00:03:09,222 --> 00:03:09,452
Rocks,

106
00:03:10,192 --> 00:03:12,272
sand and whole trees would be picked up and

107
00:03:12,272 --> 00:03:14,128
hurled across the land

108
00:03:13,858 --> 00:03:14,128
The rushing air would scour the ground and whip

109
00:03:16,388 --> 00:03:18,468
up enormous dust storms

110
00:03:18,237 --> 00:03:18,507
The sky could turn dark with dust for days

111
00:03:21,196 --> 00:03:21,466
Is there anywhere safe?

112
00:03:23,398 --> 00:03:25,638
Only places near the poles would escape the

113
00:03:25,638 --> 00:03:27,042
worst of it

114
00:03:26,622 --> 00:03:27,042
Because there,

115
00:03:27,702 --> 00:03:29,902
the ground and the air were barely moving to

116
00:03:29,902 --> 00:03:31,342
begin with

117
00:03:35,033 --> 00:03:36,807
And then there are the oceans

118
00:03:36,807 --> 00:03:38,667
They wouldn't stop either

119
00:03:38,397 --> 00:03:38,667
That's an unimaginable amount of water,

120
00:03:40,967 --> 00:03:43,567
all moving east at hundreds of miles per hour

121
00:03:43,911 --> 00:03:44,181
Giant waves would slam into the coasts and race

122
00:03:46,881 --> 00:03:48,321
far inland

123
00:03:48,069 --> 00:03:48,339
On some coasts,

124
00:03:49,319 --> 00:03:52,121
the water could pile up into waves taller than

125
00:03:52,121 --> 00:03:53,241
skyscrapers

126
00:03:53,023 --> 00:03:53,293
Cities by the sea would simply be swallowed

127
00:03:56,026 --> 00:03:56,296
And on the other side of each ocean,

128
00:03:58,116 --> 00:04:00,716
the water would rush away from the shore

129
00:04:00,510 --> 00:04:00,780
Even the solid ground would feel it,

130
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:05,240
with powerful earthquakes all around the world

131
00:04:05,402 --> 00:04:05,672
Earth's rock isn't perfectly rigid

132
00:04:07,966 --> 00:04:10,566
A sudden stop would shake the whole planet

133
00:04:10,419 --> 00:04:10,689
Volcanoes could be shaken awake

134
00:04:12,889 --> 00:04:13,159
And remember...

135
00:04:14,715 --> 00:04:17,315
this is only the first day

136
00:04:20,833 --> 00:04:22,697
So what happens next?

137
00:04:22,697 --> 00:04:23,497
Without the spin,

138
00:04:23,497 --> 00:04:25,897
the Sun would stop rising and setting every

139
00:04:25,897 --> 00:04:26,793
day

140
00:04:26,523 --> 00:04:26,793
Instead,

141
00:04:27,413 --> 00:04:28,733
it would hang in the sky,

142
00:04:28,733 --> 00:04:30,813
almost frozen in place

143
00:04:30,623 --> 00:04:30,893
As the Earth travels around the Sun,

144
00:04:32,833 --> 00:04:34,867
a single day would last...

145
00:04:34,867 --> 00:04:36,287
a whole year

146
00:04:36,017 --> 00:04:36,287
The Sun would creep across the sky,

147
00:04:38,107 --> 00:04:40,707
taking months to go from sunrise to sunset

148
00:04:41,083 --> 00:04:41,353
About six months of daylight

149
00:04:43,251 --> 00:04:45,324
Then six months of night

150
00:04:45,054 --> 00:04:45,324
One sunrise a year

151
00:04:47,006 --> 00:04:48,766
And one sunset

152
00:04:48,506 --> 00:04:48,776
The day side would bake under a Sun that

153
00:04:50,876 --> 00:04:52,271
never sets

154
00:04:52,040 --> 00:04:52,271
Temperatures there could climb higher than in

155
00:04:54,290 --> 00:04:55,974
any desert today

156
00:04:55,704 --> 00:04:55,974
Meanwhile,

157
00:04:56,514 --> 00:04:59,114
the night side would freeze in total darkness

158
00:04:59,219 --> 00:04:59,639
Without sunlight for months,

159
00:05:01,139 --> 00:05:03,739
it could get colder than Antarctica

160
00:05:07,267 --> 00:05:09,544
But here's something most people don't know

161
00:05:09,544 --> 00:05:11,964
The Earth isn't a perfect ball

162
00:05:11,694 --> 00:05:11,964
Because it spins,

163
00:05:13,024 --> 00:05:14,504
it bulges around the middle,

164
00:05:14,504 --> 00:05:16,584
by about twenty-one kilometers

165
00:05:16,731 --> 00:05:17,001
The spin flings the oceans outward too,

166
00:05:19,341 --> 00:05:21,661
so there's extra water piled up around the

167
00:05:21,661 --> 00:05:22,758
equator

168
00:05:22,488 --> 00:05:22,758
In fact,

169
00:05:23,418 --> 00:05:24,818
if you stand on the equator,

170
00:05:24,818 --> 00:05:26,818
you're farther from the Earth's center than you

171
00:05:26,818 --> 00:05:28,586
would be at the poles

172
00:05:28,316 --> 00:05:28,586
So without the spin,

173
00:05:29,646 --> 00:05:31,474
where does all that water go?

174
00:05:31,474 --> 00:05:33,554
It slowly flows away from the equator,

175
00:05:33,554 --> 00:05:35,274
toward the poles

176
00:05:35,004 --> 00:05:35,274
The solid rock would settle too,

177
00:05:37,014 --> 00:05:39,483
but far more slowly than the water

178
00:05:39,213 --> 00:05:39,483
Two giant oceans would form,

179
00:05:41,223 --> 00:05:43,517
one at each end of the planet

180
00:05:43,247 --> 00:05:43,517
The northern ocean would swallow much of

181
00:05:45,617 --> 00:05:46,177
Canada,

182
00:05:46,177 --> 00:05:47,937
Europe and Russia

183
00:05:47,684 --> 00:05:47,954
And around the middle,

184
00:05:48,974 --> 00:05:50,894
a single supercontinent would appear,

185
00:05:50,894 --> 00:05:53,494
wrapping all the way around the world

186
00:05:53,106 --> 00:05:53,526
Underwater mountains and valleys would rise

187
00:05:55,946 --> 00:05:58,026
into the open air

188
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:02,660
Here's another strange one:

189
00:06:02,660 --> 00:06:04,210
no more hurricanes

190
00:06:04,210 --> 00:06:05,192
Why?

191
00:06:05,192 --> 00:06:07,152
Because the Earth's spin is what makes big

192
00:06:07,152 --> 00:06:08,592
storms twist

193
00:06:08,389 --> 00:06:08,659
It's called the Coriolis effect

194
00:06:10,885 --> 00:06:11,155
It's also why storms in the north spin one

195
00:06:13,415 --> 00:06:13,655
way,

196
00:06:13,655 --> 00:06:16,255
and storms in the south spin the other

197
00:06:16,083 --> 00:06:16,353
Without it,

198
00:06:16,853 --> 00:06:19,333
hurricanes and typhoons as we know them would

199
00:06:19,333 --> 00:06:20,453
disappear

200
00:06:20,279 --> 00:06:20,549
Instead,

201
00:06:21,169 --> 00:06:23,329
hot air from the day side would flow toward

202
00:06:23,329 --> 00:06:25,180
the freezing night side

203
00:06:24,910 --> 00:06:25,180
And cold air from the night side would rush

204
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:29,122
back along the ground

205
00:06:28,892 --> 00:06:29,122
Winds would blow in completely new directions

206
00:06:32,230 --> 00:06:32,500
Huge clouds and heavy rain could form above the

207
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:35,880
hottest spot,

208
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:38,042
right where the Sun is overhead

209
00:06:37,772 --> 00:06:38,042
The weather would be unlike anything humans

210
00:06:40,262 --> 00:06:41,898
have ever seen

211
00:06:41,478 --> 00:06:41,898
In fact,

212
00:06:42,518 --> 00:06:45,118
scientists study faraway planets that spin very

213
00:06:45,118 --> 00:06:45,678
slowly,

214
00:06:45,678 --> 00:06:48,278
to learn what this kind of weather is like

215
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:54,420
But the scariest change might be one you can't

216
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:55,269
even see

217
00:06:55,269 --> 00:06:56,389
Deep inside the Earth,

218
00:06:56,389 --> 00:06:58,989
molten metal flows in the outer core

219
00:06:58,752 --> 00:06:59,022
This liquid iron is nearly as hot as the

220
00:07:01,522 --> 00:07:03,158
surface of the Sun

221
00:07:02,888 --> 00:07:03,158
As it moves,

222
00:07:03,978 --> 00:07:05,818
it works like a giant generator,

223
00:07:05,818 --> 00:07:08,418
creating a magnetic field around the planet

224
00:07:08,688 --> 00:07:08,958
And scientists think the planet's spin helps

225
00:07:11,378 --> 00:07:13,739
keep that magnetic field strong

226
00:07:13,469 --> 00:07:13,739
So without the spin,

227
00:07:14,919 --> 00:07:17,315
the field could become much weaker

228
00:07:17,535 --> 00:07:18,772
Want proof?

229
00:07:18,772 --> 00:07:20,065
Look at Venus

230
00:07:20,065 --> 00:07:21,065
It barely spins,

231
00:07:21,065 --> 00:07:23,505
taking two hundred and forty-three Earth days

232
00:07:23,505 --> 00:07:24,305
to turn once,

233
00:07:24,305 --> 00:07:26,905
and it has almost no magnetic field

234
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:27,070
That magnetic field is our shield

235
00:07:29,299 --> 00:07:31,659
It protects us from particles blasted out by

236
00:07:31,659 --> 00:07:32,780
the Sun

237
00:07:32,510 --> 00:07:32,780
Without it,

238
00:07:33,480 --> 00:07:34,920
over a very long time,

239
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:37,200
the solar wind could strip away some of our

240
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:38,069
air

241
00:07:37,799 --> 00:07:38,069
Compasses might stop working,

242
00:07:39,689 --> 00:07:41,529
and the northern lights could look very

243
00:07:41,529 --> 00:07:42,649
different

244
00:07:42,351 --> 00:07:42,771
And birds and sea turtles that use the magnetic

245
00:07:45,551 --> 00:07:48,151
field to find their way could get lost

246
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:52,280
So,

247
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:54,032
could anyone survive?

248
00:07:54,032 --> 00:07:55,512
If the stop was instant,

249
00:07:55,512 --> 00:07:57,712
almost nothing on the surface would last the

250
00:07:57,712 --> 00:07:59,421
first few hours

251
00:07:59,151 --> 00:07:59,421
The safest place to be?

252
00:08:01,510 --> 00:08:03,150
Near the North or South Pole,

253
00:08:03,150 --> 00:08:05,523
where the ground was barely moving

254
00:08:05,253 --> 00:08:05,523
Scientists at a research station in the Arctic

255
00:08:08,183 --> 00:08:10,594
might feel only a gentle jolt

256
00:08:10,324 --> 00:08:10,594
People deep underground,

257
00:08:11,894 --> 00:08:13,174
in strong shelters,

258
00:08:13,174 --> 00:08:15,043
might also make it

259
00:08:14,773 --> 00:08:15,043
Deep mines or bunkers could protect them from

260
00:08:17,463 --> 00:08:19,771
the winds and flying debris

261
00:08:19,501 --> 00:08:19,771
But then what?

262
00:08:21,319 --> 00:08:23,473
They'd face months of darkness...

263
00:08:23,473 --> 00:08:25,774
or months of burning sunlight

264
00:08:25,504 --> 00:08:25,774
Growing food would be incredibly hard,

265
00:08:27,794 --> 00:08:30,394
with crops either frozen or scorched

266
00:08:30,304 --> 00:08:30,574
Survivors would have to live along the edge

267
00:08:32,954 --> 00:08:34,912
between day and night

268
00:08:34,492 --> 00:08:34,912
In that narrow twilight zone,

269
00:08:36,532 --> 00:08:38,937
the temperature might be just right

270
00:08:38,667 --> 00:08:38,937
But as the Earth moved around the Sun,

271
00:08:40,997 --> 00:08:42,618
that zone would move too

272
00:08:42,618 --> 00:08:45,218
About seventy miles every single day

273
00:08:49,333 --> 00:08:49,813
Okay,

274
00:08:49,813 --> 00:08:52,310
but what if the Earth didn't stop suddenly?

275
00:08:52,310 --> 00:08:53,950
What if it slowed down gently,

276
00:08:53,950 --> 00:08:56,030
over thousands of years?

277
00:08:55,776 --> 00:08:56,046
Then there would be no giant winds,

278
00:08:57,786 --> 00:08:59,822
and no killer waves

279
00:08:59,552 --> 00:08:59,822
The oceans would still slowly flow toward the

280
00:09:02,202 --> 00:09:02,682
poles,

281
00:09:02,682 --> 00:09:05,237
as the bulge around the middle shrank

282
00:09:04,967 --> 00:09:05,237
But the days would get longer,

283
00:09:06,857 --> 00:09:08,297
and longer

284
00:09:08,044 --> 00:09:08,314
First,

285
00:09:08,734 --> 00:09:10,408
days of thirty hours

286
00:09:10,408 --> 00:09:12,226
Then a hundred hours

287
00:09:12,226 --> 00:09:13,666
Then weeks

288
00:09:13,438 --> 00:09:13,708
Our body clocks are tuned to a twenty-four-hour

289
00:09:16,328 --> 00:09:16,608
day,

290
00:09:16,608 --> 00:09:19,145
so humans would have to adapt too

291
00:09:18,875 --> 00:09:19,145
Plants and animals would have to cope with very

292
00:09:21,645 --> 00:09:22,445
long days,

293
00:09:22,445 --> 00:09:24,381
and very long nights

294
00:09:24,111 --> 00:09:24,381
Many plants open and close their leaves with

295
00:09:26,681 --> 00:09:27,161
the Sun,

296
00:09:27,161 --> 00:09:29,761
and couldn't survive weeks of darkness

297
00:09:29,567 --> 00:09:29,837
Many species would struggle to survive

298
00:09:32,510 --> 00:09:32,930
But humans might make it,

299
00:09:34,350 --> 00:09:35,430
with greenhouses,

300
00:09:35,430 --> 00:09:37,510
lights and underground farms

301
00:09:41,533 --> 00:09:41,986
And now,

302
00:09:41,986 --> 00:09:43,327
here's the twist

303
00:09:43,327 --> 00:09:45,250
The Earth really is slowing down

304
00:09:45,250 --> 00:09:46,661
Right now

305
00:09:46,391 --> 00:09:46,661
The Moon's gravity pulls on our oceans,

306
00:09:48,961 --> 00:09:51,278
and acts like a tiny brake

307
00:09:51,008 --> 00:09:51,278
The tides drag against the spinning planet,

308
00:09:53,618 --> 00:09:55,755
stealing a little of its speed

309
00:09:55,485 --> 00:09:55,755
That energy pushes the Moon farther away,

310
00:09:58,175 --> 00:10:00,575
about four centimeters every year

311
00:10:00,389 --> 00:10:00,659
Each century,

312
00:10:01,259 --> 00:10:03,759
a day gets longer by about two thousandths of

313
00:10:03,759 --> 00:10:04,935
a second

314
00:10:04,665 --> 00:10:04,935
That's one reason scientists have sometimes

315
00:10:07,195 --> 00:10:09,795
added a leap second to our clocks

316
00:10:09,535 --> 00:10:09,805
Long ago,

317
00:10:10,505 --> 00:10:12,105
when the first animals appeared,

318
00:10:12,105 --> 00:10:14,705
a day lasted only about twenty-two hours

319
00:10:15,079 --> 00:10:15,349
And even earlier,

320
00:10:16,329 --> 00:10:17,589
when the Moon had just formed,

321
00:10:17,589 --> 00:10:20,189
a day may have lasted only a few hours

322
00:10:20,533 --> 00:10:20,803
How do we know?

323
00:10:22,299 --> 00:10:23,756
Fossil corals

324
00:10:23,756 --> 00:10:26,156
Their growth rings record more days in each

325
00:10:26,156 --> 00:10:26,516
year,

326
00:10:26,516 --> 00:10:29,088
hundreds of millions of years ago

327
00:10:28,818 --> 00:10:29,088
But don't panic

328
00:10:30,558 --> 00:10:32,438
It would take far longer than the Sun's

329
00:10:32,438 --> 00:10:35,038
lifetime for the Earth to stop

330
00:10:38,567 --> 00:10:38,807
So,

331
00:10:38,807 --> 00:10:41,407
could the Earth ever suddenly stop spinning?

332
00:10:41,807 --> 00:10:42,601
No

333
00:10:42,601 --> 00:10:44,641
There's nothing in our solar system that could

334
00:10:44,641 --> 00:10:46,363
stop it all at once

335
00:10:46,093 --> 00:10:46,363
Even a giant asteroid hit would barely change

336
00:10:48,943 --> 00:10:50,696
how fast it turns

337
00:10:50,426 --> 00:10:50,696
Our planet has been spinning for about four and

338
00:10:53,076 --> 00:10:54,851
a half billion years

339
00:10:54,581 --> 00:10:54,851
It got that spin when it formed,

340
00:10:56,471 --> 00:10:58,911
from a swirling cloud of gas and dust around

341
00:10:58,911 --> 00:11:00,255
the young Sun

342
00:10:59,985 --> 00:11:00,255
And in space,

343
00:11:01,235 --> 00:11:02,715
with nothing to slow it down,

344
00:11:02,715 --> 00:11:05,315
a spinning planet just keeps on spinning

345
00:11:05,223 --> 00:11:05,493
It will keep spinning for billions of years

346
00:11:07,593 --> 00:11:08,456
more

347
00:11:08,186 --> 00:11:08,456
That spin gives us day and night,

348
00:11:10,276 --> 00:11:12,399
our winds and our weather

349
00:11:12,129 --> 00:11:12,399
It's one of the quiet reasons life on Earth

350
00:11:14,619 --> 00:11:16,275
is possible at all

351
00:11:16,005 --> 00:11:16,275
So tonight,

352
00:11:17,015 --> 00:11:18,095
when the Sun sets,

353
00:11:18,095 --> 00:11:18,615
remember:

354
00:11:18,615 --> 00:11:20,603
it's not the Sun that's moving

355
00:11:20,603 --> 00:11:22,043
It's you

356
00:11:23,067 --> 00:11:24,835
A thousand miles per hour

357
00:11:24,835 --> 00:11:26,499
A day that lasts a year

358
00:11:26,499 --> 00:11:27,299
Two oceans,

359
00:11:27,299 --> 00:11:29,059
and one supercontinent

360
00:11:29,041 --> 00:11:29,091
All of it depends on one simple thing:

361
00:11:31,291 --> 00:11:33,338
the spin beneath your feet

362
00:11:33,238 --> 00:11:33,338
Which what-if should I explore next?

363
00:11:35,557 --> 00:11:35,607
Tell me in the comments

364
00:11:37,243 --> 00:11:37,293
And subscribe,

365
00:11:38,193 --> 00:11:40,793
so you don't miss the next one
